1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,965
Lesson 0.2.

2
00:00:02,345 --> 00:00:04,240
A map of computing paradigms.

3
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:10,484
Classical, quantum, neuromorphic,
analog, reversible, thermodynamic.

4
00:00:10,864 --> 00:00:15,520
There are many ways to compute, and
each one exploits physics differently.

5
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:19,068
In this lesson we will
build a map to place them.

6
00:00:19,448 --> 00:00:21,949
The question that organizes
the map is simple:

7
00:00:22,329 --> 00:00:24,897
what does each paradigm
do with fluctuations?

8
00:00:26,017 --> 00:00:29,756
Suppose you need to flip a
fair coin a million times.

9
00:00:30,136 --> 00:00:34,108
A digital computer cannot flip
coins: it simulates them with a

10
00:00:34,108 --> 00:00:38,272
pseudorandom generator, a long
chain of deterministic operations.

11
00:00:38,652 --> 00:00:42,747
A small physical system, by contrast,
is already jiggling from heat.

12
00:00:43,127 --> 00:00:45,428
A particle in a double well with a low

13
00:00:45,428 --> 00:00:47,971
barrier hops from side to side on its own.

14
00:00:48,351 --> 00:00:51,190
Just looking at it gives random bits.

15
00:00:51,570 --> 00:00:54,592
The digital paradigm spends
energy to remove noise

16
00:00:54,592 --> 00:00:57,434
and then more energy to
manufacture randomness.

17
00:00:57,814 --> 00:01:01,125
The thermodynamic paradigm
uses the noise directly.

18
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:05,206
The variable that decides
everything is the barrier

19
00:01:05,206 --> 00:01:07,872
height compared with the
thermal energy k_B T.

20
00:01:08,252 --> 00:01:14,233
The probability of a spontaneous
hop scales as e^(−E_b/k_B T).

21
00:01:14,613 --> 00:01:18,265
With a barrier of one or
two k_B T, the particle

22
00:01:18,265 --> 00:01:21,688
hops constantly: we have
a probabilistic bit.

23
00:01:22,068 --> 00:01:24,856
If we raise the barrier, hops become rare.

24
00:01:25,236 --> 00:01:29,605
At about forty k_B T, the error
probability falls below one

25
00:01:29,605 --> 00:01:33,604
in ten quadrillion per
attempt: we have a digital bit.

26
00:01:33,984 --> 00:01:36,741
The same device can be
deterministic or random

27
00:01:36,741 --> 00:01:39,917
depending on how much energy
we put into the barrier.

28
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:44,240
We can draw a map with
two dimensionless ratios.

29
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:48,676
On the horizontal axis, the
signal energy divided by k_B T.

30
00:01:49,056 --> 00:01:55,047
On the vertical axis, the energy
of a quantum, ħω, divided by k_B T.

31
00:01:55,427 --> 00:01:59,567
Bottom right, with signals much
larger than thermal noise, lives

32
00:01:59,567 --> 00:02:03,255
classical digital computing:
fluctuations are suppressed.

33
00:02:03,635 --> 00:02:07,363
Bottom left, with signals
on the order of k_B T, lives

34
00:02:07,363 --> 00:02:11,506
thermodynamic computing: thermal
fluctuations are harnessed.

35
00:02:11,886 --> 00:02:15,999
At the top, when the energy
quantum exceeds the thermal energy,

36
00:02:15,999 --> 00:02:20,307
quantum fluctuations dominate: the
territory of quantum computing.

37
00:02:20,687 --> 00:02:22,942
These are the three fluctuation regimes.

38
00:02:24,050 --> 00:02:25,094
Let's put in numbers.

39
00:02:25,474 --> 00:02:29,528
At three hundred kelvin,
k_B T is about 4.14 ×

40
00:02:29,528 --> 00:02:33,230
10⁻²¹ J, or twenty-six millielectronvolts.

41
00:02:33,610 --> 00:02:37,531
A typical superconducting qubit
operates near five gigahertz.

42
00:02:37,911 --> 00:02:40,701
Its quantum, h f, divided by k_B,

43
00:02:40,701 --> 00:02:44,844
corresponds to a temperature
of only 0.24 kelvin.

44
00:02:45,224 --> 00:02:48,761
For thermal noise not to
destroy the quantum state, it

45
00:02:48,761 --> 00:02:52,298
must be cooled far below
that, to tens of millikelvin.

46
00:02:52,678 --> 00:02:55,812
Thermodynamic computing,
instead, works at room

47
00:02:55,812 --> 00:02:58,947
temperature, because
noise is its raw material.

48
00:03:00,050 --> 00:03:02,748
Now let's place each
paradigm by its primitive.

49
00:03:03,128 --> 00:03:06,587
Classical computing executes
deterministic Boolean logic.

50
00:03:06,967 --> 00:03:10,123
Analog computing lets
continuous quantities, like

51
00:03:10,123 --> 00:03:13,600
voltages and currents, solve
equations as they evolve.

52
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:16,755
Reversible computing uses
gates that do not erase

53
00:03:16,755 --> 00:03:19,191
information to approach
the Landauer limit.

54
00:03:19,571 --> 00:03:22,401
Neuromorphic computing
mimics the brain, with

55
00:03:22,401 --> 00:03:24,979
sparse events and memory next to compute.

56
00:03:25,359 --> 00:03:27,630
Quantum computing
manipulates amplitudes with

57
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:29,749
unitary evolution and measures at the end.

58
00:03:30,129 --> 00:03:33,414
And thermodynamic computing
lets a physical system relax

59
00:03:33,414 --> 00:03:36,876
toward a Boltzmann distribution,
and reads samples from it.

60
00:03:37,983 --> 00:03:39,883
No paradigm wins at everything.

61
00:03:40,263 --> 00:03:44,465
Digital remains unbeatable when
we need exactness and control.

62
00:03:44,845 --> 00:03:50,441
Thermodynamic targets tasks that are
already probabilistic: sampling energy-based

63
00:03:50,441 --> 00:03:55,621
models, generating with diffusion,
solving linear systems by equilibration.

64
00:03:56,001 --> 00:04:01,000
Quantum shines on problems with
interference structure, such as factoring.

65
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:04,302
And in practice systems
will be heterogeneous,

66
00:04:04,302 --> 00:04:07,669
combining several of these
paradigms in one computer.

67
00:04:08,783 --> 00:04:11,099
We will come back to this map many times.

68
00:04:11,479 --> 00:04:15,182
In unit one we will study the
thermodynamics of information.

69
00:04:15,562 --> 00:04:17,921
In unit four we will see the pbit, the

70
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:20,775
primitive of discrete
thermodynamic computing.

71
00:04:21,155 --> 00:04:24,061
In unit five we will meet real hardware.

72
00:04:24,441 --> 00:04:27,168
And in unit seven we
will compare a diffusion

73
00:04:27,168 --> 00:04:30,379
algorithm head to head with
Shor's quantum algorithm.

74
00:04:31,483 --> 00:04:33,264
Watch out for four confusions.

75
00:04:33,644 --> 00:04:38,228
Thermodynamic computing is not quantum
at room temperature: there is no

76
00:04:38,228 --> 00:04:42,553
superposition or interference, only
classical thermal fluctuations.

77
00:04:42,933 --> 00:04:47,390
Nor is it the same as analog, which
can be completely deterministic.

78
00:04:47,770 --> 00:04:51,090
It is not the same as reversible
computing, which is about not erasing.

79
00:04:51,470 --> 00:04:54,701
And noise is not always
bad: when the task is

80
00:04:54,701 --> 00:04:57,572
sampling, noise is exactly what we need.

81
00:04:58,683 --> 00:05:01,205
In summary: each paradigm
is defined by its

82
00:05:01,205 --> 00:05:04,019
primitive and by what it
does with fluctuations.

83
00:05:04,399 --> 00:05:07,586
Digital suppresses them with
barriers of tens of k_B T.

84
00:05:07,966 --> 00:05:11,842
Thermodynamic harnesses them with
barriers on the order of k_B T.

85
00:05:12,222 --> 00:05:16,327
Quantum requires the energy quantum
to exceed the thermal energy.

86
00:05:16,707 --> 00:05:20,950
The question of the course is when
harnessing noise truly saves energy.

87
00:05:22,083 --> 00:05:27,598
To practice: compute the barrier needed
for an error rate of one in a billion.

88
00:05:27,978 --> 00:05:32,541
Find the equivalent temperature of the
quantum of a ten-gigahertz oscillator.

89
00:05:32,921 --> 00:05:35,677
Classify three systems
on the paradigm map.

90
00:05:36,058 --> 00:05:40,162
And explain why sampling is the natural
task of thermodynamic computing.
